In the context of the axios library, an interceptor is a function that is used to modify the behavior of an axios request. Interceptors can be used to add additional functionality to requests, such as handling errors, passing data, or adding authorization.

When a request is made using axios, the library first calls an interceptor function for any interceptors that are set up for that request. If an interceptor returns a value (such as an error or a modified response), that value is returned to the caller, and the request is then made using the modified value.

For example, you could set up a custom interceptor function that adds a custom header to all requests made by the axios library. Here's an example of how you could do this:


```kotlin
axios.interceptor('request', function (config) {
  config.headers['custom-header'] = 'custom value';
  return config;
});

```

In this example, the `` request `` interceptor is used to modify the headers of the request being made. The `` config `` object is returned by the interceptor function, and the `` headers `` property can be used to set or modify the headers of the request.

Interceptors can be used to add additional functionality to requests, such as handling errors, passing data, or adding authorization. By chaining together multiple interceptors, you can create complex request processing pipelines.

Here's an example of how you could use an interceptor to handle errors and redirect the user to a different page after a failed request:


```kotlin
axios.interceptor('request', function (config) {
  return config.then(function (response) {
    if (response.status === 404) {
      window.location.replace('error-page.html');
    }
    return response;
  });
});

```

In this example, the `` request `` interceptor is used to modify the response of the request being made. The `` then `` method is used to handle the response of the request, and it checks the status code of the response. If the status code is 404 (Not Found), the `` window.location.replace() `` method is called to redirect the user to a different page (`` error-page.html ``). If the status code is not 404, the response is returned as-is.

This interceptor can be used with any axios request, and it will catch any errors and redirect the user to the error page defined in the `` error-page.html `` page.

