Here's an implementation of quicksort in C:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void quick_sort(int array[], int left, int right) {
    int i = left;
    int j = right;
    int pivot = array[(left + right) / 2];

    while (i <= j) {
        while (array[i] < pivot) {
            i++;
        }

        while (array[j] > pivot) {
            j--;
        }

        if (i <= j) {
            int temp = array[i];
            array[i] = array[j];
            array[j] = temp;
            i++;
            j--;
        }
    }

    if (left < j) {
        quick_sort(array, left, j);
    }

    if (i < right) {
        quick_sort(array, i, right);
    }
}

int main() {
    int array[] = {4, 6, 2, 1, 5};
    int size = sizeof(array) / sizeof(array[0]);

    quick_sort(array, 0, size - 1);

    printf("%d\n", array[0]);

    return 0;
}
```
This implementation takes an array and two indices representing the left and right ends of the subarray to be sorted. The pivot element is chosen as the middle element of the array. The outer while loop compares each element in the subarray with the pivot and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. The inner while loop ensures that the pivot remains in the middle of the subarray as the sorting proceeds.

The function call `quick_sort(array, 0, size - 1);` in the main function sorts the array in ascending order. The sorted order is printed using `printf("%d\n", array[0]);` which outputs the first element of the sorted array.

Here's an implementation of quicksort in Python:
```python
def quicksort(array):
    if len(array) <= 1:
        return array

    pivot = array[len(array) // 2]
    left = []
    right = []

    for i in range(len(array)):
        if i == len(array) - 1:
            right.append(array[i])
        elif array[i] < pivot:
            left.append(array[i])
        else:
            right.append(array[i])

    return quicksort(left) + [pivot] + quicksort(right)
```
This implementation takes an array as input and returns a sorted array. The pivot element is chosen as the middle element of the array. The outer for loop creates two subarrays, one containing all the elements to the left of the pivot and the other containing all the elements to the right of the pivot. The function then recursively sorts these subarrays and concatenates them with the pivot element in the middle to form the final sorted array.

