The `useCallback` hook in React is used to optimize the performance of your React components by memoizing a function.

Here's how it works:

1. First, you import the `useCallback` hook from the React library:
```python
import useCallback from 'react';
```
2. Then, you call the `useCallback` hook inside your component function, and pass it a function as an argument. This function will be memoized by React, which means that it will only be re-created if its dependencies change.
```scss
function MyComponent() {
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    // Do something when the button is clicked
  }, []); // empty array as dependency list, meaning handleClick will never change

  return (
    <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me!</button>
  );
}
```
3. When the component is rendered, React will create the `handleClick` function and memoize it. If the `handleClick` function is not re-created, React will simply return a reference to the same function object on subsequent renders, even if its dependencies have changed.
4. If any of the dependencies of the memoized function change, React will re-create the function object and memoize it again. This can help optimize performance by reducing unnecessary re-renders.
5. To clean up the memoized function, you can use the `useEffect` hook to call a function that will remove the memoized function from the React context. This is useful if you want to avoid memory leaks.
```scss
function MyComponent() {
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    // Do something when the button is clicked
  }, []); // empty array as dependency list, meaning handleClick will never change

  useEffect(() => {
    // Remove the handleClick function from the React context when the component is unmounted
    return () => {
      React.deleteRef(handleClick);
    };
  }, [handleClick]);

  return (
    <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me!</button>
  );
}
```
