```c
#include <stdio.h>

void bubble_sort(int arr[], int n) {
    int i, j, temp;

    // Traverse through all elements in the array
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        // Last i elements are already in place
        // Starting index i
        // Traverse through all elements from i to 0
        // Compare adjacent elements and swap if they are in the wrong order
        for (j = i; j >= 0; j--) {
            // If the current element is larger than the next one, swap them
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {5, 2, 6, 1, 3};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    bubble_sort(arr, n);
    printf("%d %d %d %d %d\n", arr[0], arr[1], arr[2], arr[3], arr[4]);
    return 0;
}
```
This code defines a function called `bubble_sort` that takes an array and its size as input, and then repeatedly compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. The function repeats this process until no more swaps are needed, which means the array is sorted.

