```python
def p1(arr, x):
    if len(arr) == 0:
        return -1
    return p2(arr, 0, len(arr) - 1, x)

def p2(arr, start, end, x):
    if start == end:
        return -1
    mid = (start + end) // 2
    if x == arr[mid]:
        return mid
    if x < arr[mid]:
        return p2(arr, start, mid - 1, x)
    return p2(arr, mid + 1, end, x)
```

This program first calls the p1 function with the array and the target element x as inputs. p1 calls the p2 function with start equal to 0 and end equal to the length of the array as inputs. p2 is a recursive function that takes three arguments: start, end, and x.

p2 first checks if start == end. If so, it means that the searched element is not found in the array, so it returns -1.

If start != end, p2 calculates the midpoint of the array from start to end and stores it in the variable mid.

p2 then compares x with arr[mid]. If x == arr[mid], it means that the searched element is found at position mid, so it returns mid.

If x < arr[mid], it means that the searched element is not present in the left half of the array, so p2 recursively calls p2 with start and mid - 1 as inputs. This ensures that the left half of the array is searched.

If x > arr[mid], it means that the searched element is not present in the right half of the array, so p2 recursively calls p2 with mid + 1 and end as inputs. This ensures that the right half of the array is searched.

The process continues until the searched element is found or until the entire array is searched.

p1 finally returns the position of the searched element, if found, or -1 if not found.

This program implements the binary search algorithm in a recursive way, which is a very efficient way to search for an element in an array

