Here's a Python program that implements the binary search algorithm:

```python
def binary_search(arr, x):
    if len(arr) == 0:
        return -1
    if len(arr) == 1:
        return x == arr[0]
    mid = len(arr) // 2
    if x == arr[mid]:
        return mid
    if x < arr[mid]:
        return binary_search(arr[0:mid], x)
    return binary_search(arr[mid+1:], x)
```

In this program, the binary_search function takes two arguments: an array arr and an element x to search for. The function first checks if the array is empty or has only one element. In both cases, it returns -1 to indicate that the element is not present.

If the array has more than one element, the function divides the array in half and finds the midpoint. It then checks if the element at the midpoint is equal to the search element. If it is, the function returns the index of the element.

If the element is not at the midpoint, the function uses the binary search algorithm to search the left and right halves of the array. The function recursively calls itself with the appropriate halves of the array and the search element.

The function finally returns the index of the element, if it is present, or -1 if it is not present.

